The inductance and capacitance of a three-phase transmission line depend upon the spacing between conductors, which is represented by the <strong>Geometric Mean Distance (GMD)</strong>. For a transposed three-phase line, \[L \propto \ln\left(\frac{\mathrm{GMD}}{\mathrm{GMR}}\right)\] and \[C \propto \frac{1}{\ln\left(\frac{\mathrm{GMD}}{\mathrm{GMR}}\right)}\] where <ul><li>GMD = Geometric Mean Distance between conductors,</li><li>GMR = Geometric Mean Radius of the conductor.</li></ul> Hence, \[L \propto \ln(\mathrm{GMD})\] and \[C \propto \frac{1}{\ln(\mathrm{GMD})}\] Case 1: Horizontal Configuration The conductor spacings are \[d,\;d,\;2d\] Therefore, \[\mathrm{GMD}
=
(d\times d\times2d)^{1/3}\] \[=
2^{1/3}d\] \[\approx1.26d\] Case 2: Equilateral Triangle All conductor spacings are equal. Thus, \[\mathrm{GMD}
=
(d\times d\times d)^{1/3}\] \[=d\] Comparison Comparing the two cases, \[1.26d&gt;d\] Hence, \[\mathrm{GMD}_{\text{horizontal}}
&gt;
\mathrm{GMD}_{\text{triangle}}\] Since inductance is directly proportional to the logarithm of GMD, \[L_{\text{horizontal}}
&gt;
L_{\text{triangle}}\] Therefore, after rearranging into an equilateral triangle, \[L\] decreases. Similarly, \[C
\propto
\frac1{\ln(\mathrm{GMD})}\] A decrease in GMD decreases the denominator, thereby increasing capacitance. Hence, \[C\] increases. Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{(C) Average capacitance will increase and inductance will decrease.}}\]

